{"source_id": "raw_mmlu_000108", "source_dataset": "mmlu", "source_subset": "professional_law", "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/mmlu.json", "source_original_index": 108, "source_question": "A bakery incorporated and headquartered in State A had a dispute with a mill incorporated and headquartered in State B over the quality of the flour the mill had delivered to the bakery. The bakery sued the mill in a federal court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $100,000 in damages. The contract between the bakery and the mill contained a clause designating State B courts as the sole venue for litigating disputes arising under the contract. Under precedent of the highest court in State A, forum-selection clauses are unenforceable as against public policy; under U.S. Supreme Court precedent, such clauses are enforceable. The mill has moved to transfer the case to a federal court in State B, citing the forum-selection clause in the parties' contract and asserting the facts that the flour was produced in State B and that the majority of likely witnesses are in State B. Is the court likely to grant the mill's motion?", "source_choices": ["No, because State A law treats forum- selection clauses as unenforceable.", "No, because the mill should have instead filed a motion to dismiss for improper venue.", "Yes, because federal common law makes the forum-selection clause controlling.", "Yes, because federal law governs transfers of venue, and it would be more convenient for the witnesses and parties to litigate the claim in State B."], "source_answer": "Yes, because federal law governs transfers of venue, and it would be more convenient for the witnesses and parties to litigate the claim in State B.", "candidate_id": "raw_mmlu_000108", "source_snapshot": {"dataset": "mmlu", "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/mmlu.json", "original_index": 108, "record": {"question": "A bakery incorporated and headquartered in State A had a dispute with a mill incorporated and headquartered in State B over the quality of the flour the mill had delivered to the bakery. The bakery sued the mill in a federal court in State A for breach of contract, seeking $100,000 in damages. The contract between the bakery and the mill contained a clause designating State B courts as the sole venue for litigating disputes arising under the contract. Under precedent of the highest court in State A, forum-selection clauses are unenforceable as against public policy; under U.S. Supreme Court precedent, such clauses are enforceable. The mill has moved to transfer the case to a federal court in State B, citing the forum-selection clause in the parties' contract and asserting the facts that the flour was produced in State B and that the majority of likely witnesses are in State B. Is the court likely to grant the mill's motion?", "choices": ["No, because State A law treats forum- selection clauses as unenforceable.", "No, because the mill should have instead filed a motion to dismiss for improper venue.", "Yes, because federal common law makes the forum-selection clause controlling.", "Yes, because federal law governs transfers of venue, and it would be more convenient for the witnesses and parties to litigate the claim in State B."], "answer": "Yes, because federal law governs transfers of venue, and it would be more convenient for the witnesses and parties to litigate the claim in State B.", "source": "MMLU", "subject": "professional_law"}}, "created_at": "2026-08-09T07:05:34.945927+00:00", "extraction": {"model": "qwen3.7-plus", "role": "TRIPLE_LABEL_MODEL", "prompt_version": "atomic-factual-triple-v6", "temperature": 0.0, "raw_response": "{\n  \"schema_version\": \"atomic-factual-triple-v6\",\n  \"extraction_status\": \"not_extractable\",\n  \"exclusion_reason\": \"multi_step_reasoning\",\n  \"subject\": null,\n  \"subject_type\": null,\n  \"relation_raw\"