{"source_id": "raw_mmlu_000248", "source_dataset": "mmlu", "source_subset": "professional_law", "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/mmlu.json", "source_original_index": 248, "source_question": "A federal employees union brought an action in federal court asking for an injunction preventing the U.S. Treasury Department from enforcing mandatory drug testing against its members who carried firearms or interdicted drugs in their official duties. When an employee in one of those categories sought a promotion, the government required him or her to undergo a program of providing urine specimens for analysis. The union claimed that these searches were in violation of the Fourth Amendment's right to privacy and to be free from unreasonable searches. The union lost the case in the lower federal court and in the Court of Appeals, but the United States Supreme Court agreed to hear it. What will the Supreme Court decide?", "source_choices": ["The drug testing policy is unconstitutional because the searches are made without probable cause or suspicion.", "The drug testing policy is unconstitutional in that it violates the employees' legitimate expectations of privacy.", "It is reasonable to conduct suspicionless testing of employees who are involved in drug interdiction or the handling of firearms.", "It is reasonable for a government employer to conduct suspicionless searches under any circumstances."], "source_answer": "It is reasonable to conduct suspicionless testing of employees who are involved in drug interdiction or the handling of firearms.", "candidate_id": "raw_mmlu_000248", "source_snapshot": {"dataset": "mmlu", "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/mmlu.json", "original_index": 248, "record": {"question": "A federal employees union brought an action in federal court asking for an injunction preventing the U.S. Treasury Department from enforcing mandatory drug testing against its members who carried firearms or interdicted drugs in their official duties. When an employee in one of those categories sought a promotion, the government required him or her to undergo a program of providing urine specimens for analysis. The union claimed that these searches were in violation of the Fourth Amendment's right to privacy and to be free from unreasonable searches. The union lost the case in the lower federal court and in the Court of Appeals, but the United States Supreme Court agreed to hear it. What will the Supreme Court decide?", "choices": ["The drug testing policy is unconstitutional because the searches are made without probable cause or suspicion.", "The drug testing policy is unconstitutional in that it violates the employees' legitimate expectations of privacy.", "It is reasonable to conduct suspicionless testing of employees who are involved in drug interdiction or the handling of firearms.", "It is reasonable for a government employer to conduct suspicionless searches under any circumstances."], "answer": "It is reasonable to conduct suspicionless testing of employees who are involved in drug interdiction or the handling of firearms.", "source": "MMLU", "subject": "professional_law"}}, "created_at": "2026-08-09T14:20:39.792888+00:00", "extraction": {"model": "qwen3.7-plus", "role": "TRIPLE_LABEL_MODEL", "prompt_version": "atomic-factual-triple-v6", "temperature": 0.0, "api_model": "qwen3.7-flash-2026-07-15", "raw_response": "{\n  \"schema_version\": \"atomic-factual-triple-v6\",\n  \"extraction_status\": \"not_extractable\",\n  \"exclusion_reason\": \"causal_reasoning\",\n  \"subject\": null,\n  \"subject_type\": null,\n  \"relation_raw\": null,\n  \"answer\": null,\n  \"answer_type\": null,\n  \"canonical_fact\": null,\n  \"extraction_confidence\": 0.0\n}", "attempt_count": 1, "error": null, "program_adjustments": []}, "terminal_status": "completed", "extraction_status": "not_extractable", "exclusion_reason": "ca