{"source_id": "raw_ai2_arc_easy_000800", "source_dataset": "ai2_arc_easy", "source_subset": null, "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/ai2_arc_easy.json", "source_original_index": 800, "source_question": "Use The Periodic Table of the Elements and the information below to answer the question. Magnesium metal (Mg) is grayish-white in color and reacts actively with water. Fluorine (F2) is a greenish-yellow gas at room temperature and is a member of the halogen family. These two elements react to produce magnesium fluoride (MgF2), a chemical commonly used in making windows and lenses. How does adding heat energy affect magnesium fluoride (MgF2) molecules?", "source_choices": ["The motion of the molecules stops.", "The motion of the molecules increases.", "The motion of the molecules decreases.", "The motion of the molecules is at a constant rate."], "source_answer": "The motion of the molecules increases.", "candidate_id": "raw_ai2_arc_easy_000800", "source_snapshot": {"dataset": "ai2_arc_easy", "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/ai2_arc_easy.json", "original_index": 800, "record": {"question": "Use The Periodic Table of the Elements and the information below to answer the question. Magnesium metal (Mg) is grayish-white in color and reacts actively with water. Fluorine (F2) is a greenish-yellow gas at room temperature and is a member of the halogen family. These two elements react to produce magnesium fluoride (MgF2), a chemical commonly used in making windows and lenses. How does adding heat energy affect magnesium fluoride (MgF2) molecules?", "choices": ["The motion of the molecules stops.", "The motion of the molecules increases.", "The motion of the molecules decreases.", "The motion of the molecules is at a constant rate."], "answer": "The motion of the molecules increases.", "source": "ai2_arc"}}, "created_at": "2026-08-09T14:25:31.135244+00:00", 