{"source_id": "raw_ai2_arc_easy_001227", "source_dataset": "ai2_arc_easy", "source_subset": null, "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/ai2_arc_easy.json", "source_original_index": 1227, "source_question": "Many scientists accept the theory that excess emission of carbon dioxide from cars and industry causes a layer of gas in the upper atmosphere that traps heat. This, in turn, causes the average temperature on Earth to rise. Which of the following observations supports that theory?", "source_choices": ["The summer of 1998 was one of the hottest summer seasons on record.", "Carbon dioxide gas from cars and industry is a product of burning that produces heat.", "Carbon dioxide in the upper atmosphere reflects heat radiated from Earth's surface.", "Radiation on Earth's surface is increasing because of a hole in the ozone layer"], "source_answer": "Carbon dioxide in the upper atmosphere reflects heat radiated from Earth's surface.", "candidate_id": "raw_ai2_arc_easy_001227", "source_snapshot": {"dataset": "ai2_arc_easy", "source_path": "/Users/qiqing/learn_resource/mutil-language/pitfalls-cross-lingual/data/source/ai2_arc_easy.json", "original_index": 1227, "record": {"question": "Many scientists accept the theory that excess emission of carbon dioxide from cars and industry causes a layer of gas in the upper atmosphere that traps heat. This, in turn, causes the average temperature on Earth to rise. Which of the following observations supports that theory?", "choices": ["The summer of 1998 was one of the hottest summer seasons on record.", "Carbon dioxide gas from cars and industry is a product of burning that produces heat.", "Carbon dioxide in the upper atmosphere reflects heat radiated from Earth's surface.", "Radiation on Earth's surface is increasing because of a hole in the ozone layer"], "answer": "Carbon dioxide in the upper atmosphere reflects heat radiated from Earth's surface.", "source": "ai2_arc"}}, 